   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-6-2-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Build a dictionary of how often words reoccur using:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Word and Phrase Dictionary</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. You do build a dictionary, but the technique is called word and phrase frequencies, because it counts how often each appears.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Word and Phrase Frequencies</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Word and phrase frequencies means building a dictionary of each word or phrase and how often it appears.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Word and Phrase Library</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The technique is word and phrase frequencies: a dictionary of each word or phrase and how often it appears.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-6-2-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>N-grams refer to</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>A text can be split into sequences of words of a set length</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. N-grams split text into runs of words of a set length, like two words (bigrams) or three words (trigrams).</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>A word can be split into sequences of characters of a set length</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. In this lesson, n-grams split a text into runs of words, not a word into characters. For example, 'the quick' is a two-word n-gram.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>A text can be split into paragraphs of a set length</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. N-grams are short runs of words, like one, two, or three words, not paragraphs.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-6-2-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Sentiment analysis</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>analyzes a phrase for positivity or negativity</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Sentiment analysis checks how positive or negative a sentence or text is.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>analyzes a phrase for sentimentality</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Sentiment analysis measures how positive or negative a text is, not how sentimental it is.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>analyzes a phrase for sadness</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. It measures how positive or negative a text is, on a scale from -1.0 to 1.0, not just sadness.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
